WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.980 --> 00:00:04.198
In school mathematics, a plane is studied from
the

2
00:00:04.198 --> 00:00:07.030
point of view of geometry. In this lesson we

3
00:00:07.030 --> 00:00:09.842
will look at a plane from the point of view of

4
00:00:09.842 --> 00:00:12.410
algebra, that is, we will describe a plane

5
00:00:12.410 --> 00:00:19.180
by its equation. First, let us figure out what
the equation of a plane is.

6
00:00:19.180 --> 00:00:22.696
After that, we will look at the main kinds of
equations

7
00:00:22.696 --> 00:00:25.700
of a plane in the rectangular coordinate
system

8
00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:30.300
Oxyz of three-dimensional space.

9
00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:37.620
A plane is a geometric figure made up of
individual points. In three-dimensional

10
00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:43.560
space, each point has coordinates given by
three numbers. The equation of a plane

11
00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:50.270
sets up a relation among the coordinates of
all its points. The equation of a plane

12
00:00:50.270 --> 00:00:56.750
in the rectangular coordinate system Oxyz

13
00:00:56.750 --> 00:01:03.270
is an equation in three variables. The
coordinates of any point lying in the plane

14
00:01:03.270 --> 00:01:09.310
satisfy the equation, and the coordinates of
any other points outside the plane

15
00:01:09.310 --> 00:01:13.290
do not satisfy it.

16
00:01:14.790 --> 00:01:19.330
In a drawing, a plane can be shown as a
parallelogram; this

17
00:01:19.330 --> 00:01:26.190
creates a sense of space. In the space Oxyz,
let a point M₀ with coordinates x₀, y₀

18
00:01:26.190 --> 00:01:32.670
and z₀ lying in a plane Q, and a vector
perpendicular to this plane,

19
00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:39.290
the vector n with coordinates A, B, C, called
the normal vector, be given.

20
00:01:39.290 --> 00:01:42.209
Let us derive the equation of the plane Q.

21
00:01:42.209 --> 00:01:46.170
To do this, we take any point M with
coordinates x, y, z.

22
00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:54.800
We form the vector M₀M. The vectors n and M₀M

23
00:01:54.800 --> 00:02:00.460
are perpendicular for any position of M, so
their dot

24
00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:04.467
product is zero, that is, the sum of the
products

25
00:02:04.467 --> 00:02:07.820
of the corresponding coordinates is zero.

26
00:02:07.850 --> 00:02:11.921
The resulting equation is called the equation
of the plane

27
00:02:11.921 --> 00:02:15.150
through a point perpendicular to the vector n.

28
00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:22.240
If we expand the brackets in this equation, we
bring it to this form; this is

29
00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:24.360
the general equation of a plane.

30
00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:29.955
Let us look at an example. We need to write
the equation of the plane

31
00:02:29.955 --> 00:02:34.740
through the point A with coordinates three,
four, minus six

32
00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:41.280
perpendicular to the vector n with coordinates
seven, five, minus nine.

33
00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:48.020
We substitute them into the formula, expand
the brackets,

34
00:02:48.020 --> 00:02:54.360
collect like terms and finally get the general
equation of the plane.

35
00:02:56.080 --> 00:02:59.840
The general equation of a plane can be
complete or incomplete.

36
00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:06.200
Planes given by incomplete equations can be
parallel to the coordinate axes,

37
00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:12.580
pass through the coordinate axes, coincide
with the coordinate planes

38
00:03:12.580 --> 00:03:19.020
or be parallel to them, or pass through the
origin.

39
00:03:20.360 --> 00:03:23.760
Let us derive one more equation of a plane.

40
00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:27.160
Let the plane pass through three points M₁,

41
00:03:27.160 --> 00:03:30.820
M₂ and M₃ that do not lie on one line.

42
00:03:31.780 --> 00:03:38.700
We mark any point M in the plane with
coordinates x, y, z and

43
00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:44.000
form the vectors M₁M, M₁M₂ and M₁M₃.

44
00:03:44.740 --> 00:03:49.640
Since the vectors lie in one plane, they are
coplanar.

45
00:03:50.140 --> 00:03:56.460
We apply the coplanarity condition for three
vectors: their triple product is zero.

46
00:03:56.490 --> 00:03:59.675
We form a third-order determinant equal to
zero:

47
00:03:59.675 --> 00:04:03.390
the first row holds the coordinates of the
first vector,

48
00:04:03.390 --> 00:04:10.520
followed by the coordinates of the second and
then the third vector.

49
00:04:10.520 --> 00:04:14.880
We get the equation of the plane through three
points.

50
00:04:16.120 --> 00:04:21.190
Let us look at an example. Let points M₁, M₂
and M₃ be given.

51
00:04:21.190 --> 00:04:27.710
We need to write the equation of the plane
through these three points.

52
00:04:27.710 --> 00:04:31.336
We substitute the coordinates of the three
points into

53
00:04:31.336 --> 00:04:34.290
the equation of a plane through three points

54
00:04:34.290 --> 00:04:38.558
and get a third-order determinant that is
convenient

55
00:04:38.558 --> 00:04:42.170
to compute by expanding along the first row.

56
00:04:43.030 --> 00:04:49.010
We get the sum of the products of the
first-row entries and their cofactors.

57
00:04:49.010 --> 00:04:55.690
Then we do the calculations, expand the
brackets and set the resulting

58
00:04:55.690 --> 00:05:02.670
expression equal to zero: 28y − 7z − 49 = 0.

59
00:05:02.670 --> 00:05:11.210
This is the equation of the plane through the
three given points M₁, M₂, M₃.

60
00:05:12.780 --> 00:05:16.321
Let us look at one more equation of a plane.

61
00:05:16.321 --> 00:05:19.540
Let the plane cut off on the axes Ox, Oy

62
00:05:19.540 --> 00:05:25.850
and Oz the segments a, b and c respectively,

63
00:05:25.850 --> 00:05:32.440
that is, pass through the points A, B and C on
the coordinate axes. Then

64
00:05:32.440 --> 00:05:39.040
we can write the intercept form of the plane's
equation: x/a + y/b

65
00:05:39.040 --> 00:05:44.220
+ z/c = 1.

66
00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:49.670
Let us look at this equation in an example.

67
00:05:49.670 --> 00:05:53.020
A plane cuts off on the coordinate axes Ox

68
00:05:53.020 --> 00:05:59.580
a segment of three, on Oy four, and on Oz two.

69
00:05:59.580 --> 00:06:05.890
We need to write the equation of this plane.
By the intercept form formula

70
00:06:05.890 --> 00:06:12.500
we get the equation. Multiplying both sides of
the equation by twelve,

71
00:06:12.500 --> 00:06:16.690
we can bring it to general form. Transforming
the equation,

72
00:06:16.690 --> 00:06:21.210
we get the general equation of the plane.

73
00:06:22.330 --> 00:06:24.964
There is one more equation of a plane:

74
00:06:24.964 --> 00:06:29.330
the normal equation. To go from the general
equation of a plane

75
00:06:29.330 --> 00:06:35.150
to the normal one, multiply both sides of the
equation

76
00:06:35.440 --> 00:06:38.737
by the normalizing factor μ. The sign of the
factor,

77
00:06:38.737 --> 00:06:42.160
plus or minus, depends on the sign of D in the
general

78
00:06:42.160 --> 00:06:49.320
equation of the plane: it is taken opposite to
it.

79
00:06:50.410 --> 00:06:57.150
Suppose we need to write the general equation
3x + 2y + 6z + 12 = 0

80
00:06:57.150 --> 00:07:04.000
as a normal equation. Then we find the
normalizing factor μ.

81
00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:10.940
D equals twelve, a positive number, so note
that the sign in front of the fraction

82
00:07:10.940 --> 00:07:17.850
is minus. As a result we get the fraction one
seventh.

83
00:07:17.850 --> 00:07:23.375
Now we multiply all the terms of the general
equation

84
00:07:23.375 --> 00:07:28.170
by μ and get the normal equation of the plane.

85
00:07:30.090 --> 00:07:36.620
Using the normalizing factor, we can find the
distance from a point to a plane.

86
00:07:36.620 --> 00:07:40.140
The distance from a point M₀ to a plane Q is
the length

87
00:07:40.140 --> 00:07:43.340
of the perpendicular dropped from M₀ to the
plane.

88
00:07:43.340 --> 00:07:51.330
The distance from a point to a plane is given
by the following formula.

89
00:07:52.710 --> 00:07:59.420
Let us look at an example. Let a point M₀ with
coordinates three, five, minus six

90
00:07:59.420 --> 00:08:06.120
and the general equation of a plane 2x + 3y +
6z − 4

91
00:08:06.120 --> 00:08:09.600
= 0 be given.

92
00:08:09.640 --> 00:08:16.000
Let us find the distance from M₀ to the plane.
We substitute the given data

93
00:08:16.000 --> 00:08:22.740
into the formula and the calculation gives
twenty-seven sevenths;

94
00:08:22.740 --> 00:08:25.500
this is the distance.

95
00:08:26.880 --> 00:08:33.560
Let planes Q₁ and Q₂ intersect in space, each

96
00:08:33.560 --> 00:08:39.690
given by its general equation. To find the
angle between the planes,

97
00:08:39.690 --> 00:08:45.110
we need the coordinates of the normal vectors
n₁ and n₂.

98
00:08:45.140 --> 00:08:51.520
The angle φ between vectors n₁ and n₂ is the
angle between the original planes.

99
00:08:51.520 --> 00:08:58.120
We use the formula for the cosine of the angle
between two vectors.

100
00:08:58.120 --> 00:09:00.550
Recall that

101
00:09:00.550 --> 00:09:03.703
the numerator is the dot product of the
vectors and

102
00:09:03.703 --> 00:09:06.670
the denominator is the product of their
lengths;

103
00:09:06.670 --> 00:09:13.490
written out in full, the formula looks like
this. To find the acute angle,

104
00:09:13.490 --> 00:09:17.770
take the absolute value of the right-hand
side.

105
00:09:18.640 --> 00:09:25.350
Let us look at an example: x + 2y − 3z + 4

106
00:09:25.350 --> 00:09:29.000
= 0 and 2x

107
00:09:29.000 --> 00:09:35.340
+ 3y + z + 8 = 0. We need to find the angle

108
00:09:35.340 --> 00:09:42.080
between the planes. We write the coordinates
of the normal vectors n₁ and n₂

109
00:09:42.080 --> 00:09:48.870
and substitute them into the formula for the
cosine. We get the numerical result

110
00:09:48.870 --> 00:09:55.950
five fourteenths. Then the angle equals the
arccosine of five fourteenths.

111
00:09:58.220 --> 00:10:02.880
Whether planes are parallel or perpendicular

112
00:10:02.880 --> 00:10:09.850
is determined by their normal vectors: if two
planes are parallel,

113
00:10:09.850 --> 00:10:16.530
their normal vectors are parallel too. Then
the parallelism condition for vectors

114
00:10:16.530 --> 00:10:22.890
gives a proportion of their coordinates. In
the same way,

115
00:10:22.890 --> 00:10:29.320
for perpendicularity: planes are perpendicular
if their

116
00:10:29.320 --> 00:10:33.000
normal vectors are perpendicular,

117
00:10:33.000 --> 00:10:36.127
that is, their dot product is zero;

118
00:10:36.127 --> 00:10:41.040
writing out the formula, we get the following
equality.

119
00:10:42.610 --> 00:10:49.350
Determine whether two planes are parallel, one
given by the equation 3x − 2y

120
00:10:49.350 --> 00:10:55.810
− 3z = 0 and the other by the equation 9x

121
00:10:55.810 --> 00:11:02.070
− 6y − 9z − 5 = 0.

122
00:11:02.070 --> 00:11:08.640
We write the coordinates of the normal vectors
n₁ and n₂ and

123
00:11:08.640 --> 00:11:15.380
check the parallelism condition. As we can
see, all the fractions are equal,

124
00:11:15.380 --> 00:11:18.460
so the planes are parallel.

125
00:11:19.740 --> 00:11:26.560
We need to determine whether the planes given
by 2x − 3y + z − 2 = 0 and 4x

126
00:11:26.560 --> 00:11:33.510
+ 3y + z + 5 = 0 are perpendicular.

127
00:11:33.510 --> 00:11:40.050
We write the coordinates of the normal vectors
n₁ and n₂

128
00:11:40.050 --> 00:11:42.824
and check the perpendicularity condition.

129
00:11:42.824 --> 00:11:46.950
Substituting the coordinates of the vectors
into the formula,

130
00:11:46.950 --> 00:11:52.170
we get zero. So the planes are perpendicular.

131
00:11:53.160 --> 00:11:56.695
In this lesson we looked at different
equations of

132
00:11:56.695 --> 00:12:00.160
a plane and learned how, using elements of
vector

133
00:12:00.160 --> 00:12:10.040
algebra, to determine the relative position of
planes.
